course: dictionary
lesson: 4327
title: Grub: one word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have several meanings, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. Read the words around it first. «Start digging in a garden and a robin will hop down beside your spade, because every turn of the soil brings up worms and grubs.» Digging, soil and worms are the clues, so a grub here is a small creature that lives in the ground.
explain: Now try a different sentence. «Grub's up!» shouts Mum from the kitchen. Nobody is digging and no insects are near, so grub must mean food. Ask yourself: what word would fit here, and does my meaning still make sense when I put it in the sentence?
explain: Grub can also be a verb, an action word. «The pig grubbed for roots under the oak tree.» The pig is digging about in the ground, so to grub means to dig or search about in the ground or in a messy place. Check the verb by swapping in your meaning: the pig dug for roots, and it still makes sense.
explain: Words from other places can carry special meanings too. An Australian lesson might mention a «witchetty grub», a large grub eaten as traditional bush food by Aboriginal people. Here the word names a particular kind of grub, so a dictionary is the best way to check it.
fact: A grub is the young, worm-like form of some insects, especially beetles. It is also called a larva.
fact: Grubs live in soil or wood. «Ancient» and «rotting» are not needed here; just remember that grubs often live out of sight.
fact: In an insect life cycle, the stages are egg, larva (grub), pupa and adult.
fact: «Grub» means food in informal British, Australian and New Zealand talk, as in «Grub's up!» for a meal that is ready.
fact: «Grubby» is an adjective that means dirty. «Grub» as a verb means to dig or search about in the ground or a messy place.
exercise: «Winnie the Pooh only wants the honey, but a real bear also eats the juicy grubs.» In this sentence, what is a grub? | *A young, worm-like insect | A kind of sweet | A bee's nest | A type of bear
exercise: «Start digging in a garden and a robin will hop down beside your spade, because every turn of the soil brings up worms and grubs.» What does the robin find in the soil? | Honey and bees | Bread crusts | *Worms and grubs | Sweets and crisps
exercise: «Grub's up!» shouts Mum from the kitchen. What does it mean? | The garden needs digging | A beetle has hatched | The shop is closed | *The meal is ready
test: «The pig grubbed for roots under the oak tree.» In this sentence, grubbed means: | Washed clean | *Dug or searched about in the ground | Ran quickly away | Ate a big dinner
test: «Her trainers were grubby after the muddy game.» Grubby means: | Shiny | *Dirty | Very cold | Brand new
exam: In Australia, what is a «witchetty grub»? | A kind of tractor for ploughing fields | *A large grub eaten as traditional bush food | A stag beetle's antler | A type of crow that bends wire
